1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,587
WaterFault is a working prototype for a community coordinator who receives several reports

2
00:00:04,587 --> 00:00:09,174
about the same water service fault. The goal is simple: keep the evidence

3
00:00:09,174 --> 00:00:13,761
from every resident, connect reports that describe one issue, and make it clear

4
00:00:13,761 --> 00:00:18,348
which repair team owns the next action. This presentation uses fictional people, locations,

5
00:00:18,348 --> 00:00:22,935
and repair records. WaterFault is not connected to a utility, and none of

6
00:00:22,935 --> 00:00:27,170
the actions shown dispatches a real crew or claims a real repair.

7
00:00:28,170 --> 00:00:33,427
The starting dashboard shows one unresolved issue, two reports waiting for review, one

8
00:00:33,427 --> 00:00:38,684
active repair crew, and three original reports. These are database records, not decorative

9
00:00:38,684 --> 00:00:43,941
counters. A new report captures a reporter label, a landmark, and what was

10
00:00:43,941 --> 00:00:49,198
observed. This deliberately keeps intake small. The concept addresses a coordination problem: repeated

11
00:00:49,198 --> 00:00:54,455
reports can contain useful corroborating evidence, even when they refer to the same

12
00:00:54,455 --> 00:00:59,308
underlying fault. Removing duplicates entirely would lose that evidence and its history.

13
00:01:00,308 --> 00:01:05,130
The review inbox suggests possible matches using a simple comparison of landmark words

14
00:01:05,130 --> 00:01:09,952
and fault descriptions. It is a transparent heuristic, not an artificial intelligence model,

15
00:01:09,952 --> 00:01:14,774
and its score is not a calibrated probability. The coordinator reads the original

16
00:01:14,774 --> 00:01:19,596
report and decides whether to confirm the link. This human decision matters: two

17
00:01:19,596 --> 00:01:24,418
leaks near one market might be separate problems. The prototype leaves uncertain reports

18
00:01:24,418 --> 00:01:28,869
in review instead of automatically merging them or inventing a repair assignment.

19
00:01:29,869 --> 00:01:34,560
Here is the result of confirming the second report as a duplicate of

20
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:39,252
the market gate issue. Both original reports now appear inside the shared repair

21
00:01:39,252 --> 00:01:43,944
record. Their references, descriptions, reporters, and landmarks remain separate. The event history records

22
00:01:43,944 --> 00:01:48,635
that the coordinator linked the report after review. The number of reports waiting

23
00:01:48,635 --> 00:01:53,327
for review falls from two to one, while the total number of original

24
00:01:53,327 --> 00:01:58,018
reports remains three. Linking changes the relationship; it does not delete the resident's

25
00:01:58,018 --> 00:01:58,379
evidence.

26
00:01:59,379 --> 00:02:04,333
The repair panel initially showed Demo Crew A as the active owner. The

27
00:02:04,333 --> 00:02:09,287
interface disabled a second assignment, and the database also enforces one active assignment

28
00:02:09,287 --> 00:02:14,241
per issue. After the coordinator marks the fictional repair resolved, the issue changes

29
00:02:14,241 --> 00:02:19,195
to resolved, the active crew count falls, and the completed assignment stays in

30
00:02:19,195 --> 00:02:24,149
the record. The history preserves creation, assignment, duplicate linking, and resolution. That makes

31
00:02:24,149 --> 00:02:29,103
it possible to explain what happened without confusing an assignment with a completed

32
00:02:29,103 --> 00:02:29,484
repair.

33
00:02:30,484 --> 00:02:35,831
We then logged another fictional report and reloaded the page. The new report

34
00:02:35,831 --> 00:02:41,178
remained saved, bringing the original report count to four. Because the earlier issue

35
00:02:41,178 --> 00:02:46,524
was closed, the new report stayed unlinked for coordinator review. Persistence comes from

36
00:02:46,524 --> 00:02:51,871
a normalized SQLite database with separate reports, issues, repair crews, assignments, and event

37
00:02:51,871 --> 00:02:57,218
records. A lightweight Python server provides the application programming interface, while the browser

38
00:02:57,218 --> 00:03:02,565
presents the coordinator's workspace. The prototype runs locally without a paid external service.

39
00:03:03,565 --> 00:03:09,311
Five automated workflow and interface tests passed locally and on GitHub. The browser

40
00:03:09,311 --> 00:03:15,057
walkthrough also confirmed linking, resolution, report intake, and persistence after reload. The next

41
00:03:15,057 --> 00:03:20,803
useful step is to interview coordinators and test whether this workflow matches their

42
00:03:20,803 --> 00:03:26,549
real responsibilities, then add authentication and a supervised utility integration if warranted. No

43
00:03:26,549 --> 00:03:32,295
time savings, water savings, or adoption figures have been measured. OpenAI Codex assisted

44
00:03:32,295 --> 00:03:38,041
with code and documentation, and this narration is synthetic speech. The public repository

45
00:03:38,041 --> 00:03:41,577
contains the runnable prototype, tests, architecture, and disclosures.
